The Nautilus DevOps team is testing various Ansible modules on servers in `Stratos DC`. They're currently focusing on file creation on remote hosts using Ansible. Here are the details:

a. Create an inventory file `~/playbook/inventory` on `jump host` and include `all app servers`.

b. Create a playbook `~/playbook/playbook.yml` to create a blank file `/opt/webapp.txt` on `all app servers`.

c. Set the permissions of the `/opt/webapp.txt` file to `0755`.

d. Ensure the user/group owner of the `/opt/webapp.txt` file is `tony` on `app server 1`, `steve` on `app server 2` and `banner` on `app server 3`.

`Note:` Validation will execute the playbook using the command `ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml`, so ensure the playbook functions correctly without any additional arguments.

SOLUTION


Yes. Since the validator runs from ~/playbook with no extra variables, the cleanest solution is to use ansible_user/ansible_host from the inventory and set the owner conditionally based on the app server hostname.

On the jump host, run:

mkdir -p ~/playbook
cd ~/playbook

Create the inventory:
nano ~/playbook/inventory

Use:

[app_servers]
stapp01 ansible_user=tony ansible_password=YOUR_TONY_PASSWORD
stapp02 ansible_user=steve ansible_password=YOUR_STEVE_PASSWORD
stapp03 ansible_user=banner ansible_password=YOUR_BANNER_PASSWORD

Replace the three YOUR_..._PASSWORD values with the passwords provided by the lab.

3. Test Ansible
cd ~/playbook
ansible -i inventory app_servers -m ping

If all three return SUCCESS, run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml
Finally verify:

ansible -i inventory app_servers -m shell -a 'ls -l /opt/webapp.txt'
